  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Cros deï Pin beach (also called the port beach), allée Mireille Delfino, is one of the biggest beaches on the Saint-Jean-Cap-Ferrat peninsula, in the heart of the village. It is very family-friendly, with children's games, a beach-volley court in summer and a snack kiosk. It is covered in fine gravel, like the other beaches of the peninsula (tourist information office). A corner of it is reserved for dogs.</p>

      <h2>Dogs: the published rule</h2>
      <p>According to the Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office and Destination Côte d'Azur, a beach corner is reserved for dogs at the far end of the beach, on the left. Dogs must be kept on a leash there but are allowed to swim. The Saint-Jean-Cap-Ferrat town hall writes for its part that "dogs are allowed on part of the beach" and that the other beaches of the peninsula are off-limits to them. The Nice tourist office also specifies that outside the designated zones, dogs are not allowed on the beaches of the metropolitan area.</p>
      <p>The sources consulted publish neither a period nor hours for this zone: the tourist office listing shows the beach open all year, every day. The town hall refers to its municipal orders for the rules.</p>
      <p>Facilities: public toilets, showers, individual lockers, play area. Paid parking; access to the beach is free. Anti-jellyfish net and lifeguards in July and August. An unofficial site describes the dog zone on the Beaulieu-sur-Mer side, at the back of the beach.</p>
      <div class="callout">Check before you go: the exact period and extent of the dog-reserved corner (municipal order not read), and whether the zone is marked out on site. Contact: Saint-Jean-Cap-Ferrat tourist information office, +33 4 93 76 08 90.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Villefranche-sur-Mer is a few minutes away, with its Deux Rups beach that the tourist office also cites for dogs.</p>
    </div>

